就像，并没有理由，我们甚至可以从讨论这一点开始，我
们的脑子是为，你知道，为了狩猎采集而进化的，对吧？
Like there's no reason, like we we sort of open by discussing this this
our brain was invented for, you know, evolved for hunter-gathering, right?

94
00:09:41,888 --> 00:09:50,692
然而我们现在却在欣赏艺术、创作艺术，还有建筑
等实物，以及令人惊叹的技术，比如各种科学领域。
And yet here we are appreciating fine art and creating fine art and physical
things like architecture and amazing technologies like you know, sciences.

95
00:09:51,088 --> 00:09:53,328
所以，嗯，但这并不是它的极限。
So um, but that's not the limit of that.

96
00:09:53,568 --> 00:09:56,676
也许还有新的艺术形式等待被发掘。
Maybe there's new uh uh forms of art to be discovered.

97
00:09:56,928 --> 00:10:06,148
而且我认为，如果正确使用这些工具，它们将帮助我们去探索那个新世界，
这就是为什么我称之为科学发现与奇迹。
And I think these tools um used in the right way will help us discover
that new world of that's why I call it scientific discovery and wonder.

98
00:10:06,608 --> 00:10:13,648
嗯，所以我觉得这可能会很奇妙，而且我认为即便是在这个
领域，最好的蓝图也来自艺术，来自一些精彩的科幻作品。
Um, so I think it could be amazing, and I think even there, the best
blueprints for that come from the arts, from some amazing science fiction.

99
00:10:13,934 --> 00:10:24,548
在座的各位如果有科幻作家或剧本作家，你们知道，我们需要更多基于科学、硬科学的优秀标杆。
And any of you who are science fiction writers in the room or script writers, you know,
what we need is more examples of good North stars that we can that are grounded in science, hard science.

100
00:10:24,846 --> 00:10:30,288
这样它们才具有合理性并可能实现，但我们需要这些好的标杆。
So that they're plausible and potentially doable, but there are good North stars.

101
00:10:30,510 --> 00:10:44,900
对我来说，在成长过程中，是阿西莫夫的《基地》系列，还有伊恩·班克斯的《文化》系列，
真正启发了我所认为人类可以抵达的、令人惊叹的世界或宇宙。
And for me, when I was growing up, it was Asimov's foundation series, and it was um Ian Banks' culture series that really inspired the
sorts of world or universe that I thought could be amazing for humanity to get to.

102
00:10:45,646 --> 00:10:52,608
我的意思是，除了其他方面，丹尼斯，你非常出色地展示了为什么 RSA 如此重要。
I mean, alongside everything else, Dennis, you've done a really great job of demonstrating why the RSA is so important.

103
00:10:52,718 --> 00:10:55,328
所以我相信他们会为此感谢你的。
So I'm sure that they thank you for that.

104
00:10:56,368 --> 00:10:56,768
没错。
Exactly.

105
00:10:57,006 --> 00:10:58,768
好的，观众席里还有几个问题。
Uh okay, we have a few questions in the audience.

106
00:10:58,928 --> 00:10:59,648
凯特，你在哪？
Kate, where are you?

107
00:11:01,728 --> 00:11:02,128
谢谢。
thank you.

108
00:11:02,448 --> 00:11:05,088
凯特·瓦拉，国家剧院的 co-chief executive。
Kate Varra, the co-chief second of the National Theatre.

109
00:11:05,168 --> 00:11:05,408
谢谢。
Thank you.

110
00:11:05,488 --> 00:11:07,088
刚才的内容非常迷人。
That was completely fascinating.

111
00:11:07,278 --> 00:11:18,608
嗯，你非常有说服力地谈到了你与艺术家合作所做的工作，
去思考 AI 如何增强创造力而非取代它。
Um, you talked really uh convincingly about um the work you've been doing with
artists to think about the ways that AI can enhance creativity and not replace it.

112
00:11:18,926 --> 00:11:32,900
所以我想请问一个问题，关于你认为或者是否认为人类讲故事中存在任何不可计算的元素，
如果有的话，我们在追求创新和普及的过程中该如何保护这些元素。
And so I think I'd love to ask a question about um how you think or whether you think there are any elements of human storytelling
that are non computable, and if so, how we could go about protecting them in the rush to innovate and adopt.

113
00:11:33,742 --> 00:11:34,916
是的，很好的问题。
Yeah, great question.

114
00:11:37,358 --> 00:11:41,200
嗯，我认为这实际上是一个开放的科学问题，对吧？
Well, I think that's an open scientific question, actually, right?

115
00:11:41,390 --> 00:11:52,132
所以我会称之为一个经验性问题，即我们目前在计算机方面面临的限制是什么，
而大脑可能在做一些不同的事情。
So I would call it an empirical question in the sense of what are the limits
that we have with computers that the brain may be doing something different.

116
00:11:52,430 --> 00:11:56,324
这也是我之所以想研究神经科学的部分原因，因为我对这些差异非常着迷。
And that's partly why I wanted to study neuroscience because I was fascinated about the differences.

117
00:11:56,750 --> 00:12:10,596
我一直相信，构建像 AI 这样的智能产物并将其与人类思维进行对比，
是我们找出人类思维中哪些部分依然特殊甚至独特的最好方法。
I actually have always believed that building an intelligent artifact like AI and then comparing it to the human mind will be the best
way for us to work out what remaining things are special or perhaps even unique about the human mind.

118
00:12:11,630 --> 00:12:12,560
这是一个开放的辩论。
This is an open debate.

119
00:12:12,846 --> 00:12:19,236
如果你和罗杰·彭罗斯这样的人交谈，他会谈到大脑中的量子效应以及量子意识的概念。
If you talk to someone like Roger Penrose, he will talk about quantum effects in the brain and the idea of quantum consciousness.

120
00:12:20,206 --> 00:12:26,960
如果那是真的，那么传统的计算机，也就是目前构建的 AI 机器，就无法做到那些事情了，对吧？
if that's true, then classical computers, AI machines as they're built today, won't be able to do those things, right?

121
00:12:27,438 --> 00:12:31,364
不幸的是，在这一方面，我不确定我是否同意他的观点。
Unfortunately, on that side of the ledger, I do, I'm not sure I agree with him.

122
00:12:31,662 --> 00:12:34,000
所以到目前为止，我认为还没有任何证据。
So I don't think there's any evidence so far.

123
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:45,600
我支持艾伦·图灵的观点，即我们目前能做的大多数事情，
即便我们还不清楚原理，最终也会被证明是可计算的。
I'm on the side of Alan Turing here, which is that probably most of the things we're able to do,
even though we're not sure how we do them, will eventually be computable, shown to be computable.

124
00:12:45,902 --> 00:12:51,760
所以如果这是真的，那么只要提供足够的输入和数据，机器最终可能也能做到其中的一些事情。
So if that's true, then maybe machines will eventually be able to do some of these things if they're given enough inputs and data.

125
00:12:52,014 --> 00:12:55,012
但这并不一定意味着它会变得更好，因为什么是“更好”呢？
That doesn't necessarily mean it'll be better, because what is better?

126
00:12:55,246 --> 00:13:02,400
有些事情比如下围棋或国际象棋，你可以衡量它们是否比对手更强，对吧？
So there are some things like playing chess or go, which you can measure are they better than the other player, right?

127
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:04,560
或者它们是否比人类冠军更强等等。
Are they better than the human champion and so on?

128
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:10,820
但我认为在创意领域，并没有那种线性意义上的“更好”的概念。
But I think in the creative world, there isn't that notion of better in some linear fashion.

129
00:13:11,182 --> 00:13:12,356
所以它们只会变得不同。
So they'll just be different.

130
00:13:12,782 --> 00:13:16,676
然后我认为这最终取决于，对另一个人类来说，什么更有意义？
And then I think it comes down to well, what's more meaningful to another human being?

131
00:13:16,974 --> 00:13:32,548
我经常举的例子是：为什么在某些故事中，无论是电影还是书籍，我们还要去强调“基于真实故事”？
如果这句话没有意义，我们为什么要提它呢？
And the example I always give here is why do we, for some stories, bother, like you know, whether it's a film or a book,
sometimes it's like based on a true story, So why would we bother with that phrase if it didn't have some meaning?

132
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:41,840
我认为是因为在那些特定的故事里，观众知道另一个人
类经历过这些，真正经历过这些，对他们来说更有意义。
I think it's because in those particular stories, it's more meaningful to the audience
to know that another human being went through that, actually went through that.

133
00:13:42,158 --> 00:13:44,772
所以这就会产生一种附加效应，对吧？
So then that has some additive effect, right?

134
00:13:45,070 --> 00:13:45,440
我猜是这样。
I assume.

135
00:13:45,646 --> 00:13:48,324
否则，你为什么要提到它是不是真实的故事呢？
Otherwise, why would you mention whether it was a true story or not?

136
00:13:48,750 --> 00:13:53,956
所以这永远不会适用于由人工智能创作的艺术作品，对吧？
So that will never be true of an AI-created piece of art, right?

137
00:13:54,480 --> 00:14:05,760
因为你无法与艺术家建立那种联系。就像当我看到一幅惊人的 Van Gogh 作品时，
你能从每一笔笔触中看到他的痛苦，对吧？
So that you'll have that connection with the artist in the way when I look at an amazing Van Gogh or something,
you can see his pain in every brushstroke, right?

138
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:08,644
他画作中的每一笔。
Every stroke of his paintings.

139
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:12,560
如果是一个 AI 机器人做到了这些，那还会那么有意义吗？
would that be so meaningful if an AI robot was able to do that?

140
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:17,156
大概不会，作为同类人类，我们可能永远不会觉得它有意义。
Probably not, probably never for us as fellow human beings.

141
00:14:17,614 --> 00:14:20,676
我认为这在哲学上是需要深入探讨的问题。
But this is what needs to be unpacked, I think, philosophically.

142
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:23,972
我们有来自托马斯的另一个问题。
we have another question from Thomas.

143
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:27,280
你好。
Hello.

144
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:32,400
我是托马斯·海特里克，我们的设计师，建筑设计师。
It's Thomas Heatherwick, uh our designer, architectural designer.

145
00:14:32,654 --> 00:14:46,800
我只想说，能在伦敦，也就是英国的首都，听到你表达出的这
种乐观态度是多么令人振奋，因为目前这里有很多悲观情绪。
I just wanted to say how thrilling it is to have this optimism from you and you existing here in London, in the capital of the UK,
where there's a lot of non-optimism at the moment.

146
00:14:47,118 --> 00:14:55,876
我认为你带来了很多希望和兴奋感，而且我觉得我们现在的乐观程度远远不够。
And you give, I think, a lot of hope and excitement to and I think we're not optimistic enough at all.

147
00:14:56,800 --> 00:15:08,720
而且你决定不去加利福尼亚落户，我认为这对英国来说
是明智的，这体现了英国最核心的特质，也就是“古怪”。
and your decision to not base yourself over in California has been, I think, brilliant for the UK and sort of reflects British what I
think is the best of British, which is eccentricity.

148
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:14,436
而你毫无疑问是一个严肃的“古怪”之人，这也是最理性的状态。
And you are no question a serious eccentric, um, which is the most rational thing to be.

149
00:15:14,830 --> 00:15:28,356
嗯，但我的问题是，在拥有能把你带到加州的惊人飞机、卫星和非凡成就的同时，
我们并没有在城市建设上做出令人惊叹的成就。
Um, but I my question was just um at the same time as the amazing planes that could have taken you to California and satellites and
phenomenal achievements, we have not been doing incredible things with our cities.

150
00:15:29,070 --> 00:15:35,120
而且我们在全球各地都在大规模制造毫无灵魂的东西。
And we've been making real soullessness at scale everywhere in the world.

151
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:47,920
与此同时，早期数字革命在许多方面都非常出色，但它也造
成了诺里娜·赫茨在她的书中提到的所谓的“孤独的世纪”。
And at the same time, the earlier part of the digital revolution has been incredible in so many ways,
but it's also making what I think Noreena Hertz in her book said called the lonely century.

152
00:15:49,008 --> 00:15:55,088
还有这种联系以及人们之间相互产生的毒性行为。
And this uh this connection and um people's toxic behavior with each other.

153
00:15:55,406 --> 00:15:57,488
回到你提到的健康问题。
And back to your thing of health.

154
00:15:58,208 --> 00:16:07,300
嗯，我认为这是一个很好的机会，不再仅仅把建筑看作独立的艺术品，
而是把我们身边的建筑视为公共健康的一部分。
Uh I think this is a great opportunity to stop thinking of buildings
just as individual artworks, but buildings around us as public health.

155
00:16:07,888 --> 00:16:21,830
我很感兴趣，你认为在英国伦敦的核心地带，这些正在被开启的潜力是什么？
作为一个同时对建筑充满热情的专业人士，我们该如何彻底提升周围建筑环境的人文感？
And I'm fascinated as to what you see the potential is with these sort of which you are unlocking here in Britain in the heart of
London, for how could we radically humanize the built world around us

156
00:16:21,830 --> 00:16:27,088
我很感兴趣，你认为在英国伦敦的核心地带，这些正在被开启的潜力是什么？
作为一个同时对建筑充满热情的专业人士，我们该如何彻底提升周围建筑环境的人文感？
with someone like you who's passionate about buildings at the same time?

157
00:16:27,488 --> 00:16:33,808
是的，非常感谢这个提问。也要感谢那些令人惊叹的新建筑，它们非常有启发性。
Yes, well, um thanks so much for that question and also thanks for our amazing new buildings, you know, it's very inspiring.

158
00:16:33,888 --> 00:16:40,928
我非常相信物理空间能激发更高层次的创造力。
And I'm a big believer in your physical spaces, inspiring your next level of you know creativity.

159
00:16:41,088 --> 00:16:44,452
所以我一直认为这一点极其重要。
So I've always thought that's unbelievably important.

160
00:16:44,718 --> 00:16:53,328
正如我们讨论过的，我认为必须对物理世界和建筑
采取行动，特别是在大城市里，让它们具有启发性。
And as we've discussed, I think something needs to be done about the
physical world and architecture, especially in big cities, to be inspiring.

161
00:16:53,550 --> 00:16:57,328
而从某种程度上说，自从二战以后，我们就失去了这一点。
And we somehow lost that since, you know, you could argue, since World War II.

162
00:16:57,678 --> 00:16:59,488
我最喜欢的建筑风格是 Art Deco。
And my favorite type of architecture is Art Deco.

163
00:16:59,888 --> 00:17:05,568
不过你知道，在伦敦到处都能看到很多漂亮的乔治亚风格演变。
And but you know, you can see many beautiful evolutions of that, Georgian, all around London, actually.

164
00:17:05,771 --> 00:17:07,204
嗯，但后来这种发展似乎停滞了。
Um, and then it's kind of stopped.

165
00:17:07,534 --> 00:17:11,684
这种情况挺奇怪的，而且不应该变成这样。
And it's sort of strange why that is, and um, it shouldn't be like that.

166
00:17:11,982 --> 00:17:21,328
我认为进入一个资源更丰富的时代的一个好处是，
我们可能不必只把成本作为唯一的衡量标准，对吧？
And I think um one of the benefits of entering maybe an era where there's more
abundance is we might not have to just use the the cost as the only measure, right?

167
00:17:21,406 --> 00:17:22,528
我们之前讨论过这个。
And we've discussed this before.

168
00:17:22,608 --> 00:17:24,448
不应该只用成本来衡量你建造的东西。
It shouldn't be the only measure of what you build.

169
00:17:24,608 --> 00:17:28,928
我们还应该考虑关于人类健康和灵感的其他因素。
There should we should also factor in other factors about human health, human inspiration.

170
00:17:29,008 --> 00:17:34,692
在设计下一个公屋或类似的建筑时，这些因素是如何被纳入考量的？
How is that being factored into our next design of the next council estate or or or whatever that is?

171
00:17:35,008 --> 00:17:44,768
而且正如我们一直在谈论的，我们将可能会在一些领域进行合作，
比如现在有很多新建筑正在兴建，比如数据中心以及其他与 AI 革命相关的设施。
And uh and and and as we've been talking about, we'll probably collaborate on things like there's a lot of new buildings going up,
like data centers and other things to do with the new AI revolution.

172
00:17:44,848 --> 00:17:48,452
嗯，也许我们应该多花一点钱，把它们造得漂亮点。
Well, maybe we should just spend a little bit more and make them beautiful.

173
00:17:48,718 --> 00:17:53,412
你知道的，这些是我们要建造的新型资产。
What you know, these are the new uh uh uh types of assets that we're building.

174
00:17:53,648 --> 00:17:59,300
我们为什么不考虑它们对物理环境的影响呢？
Why should we not, you know, we should be thinking about their effect on the physical environment uh as well.

175
00:17:59,630 --> 00:18:11,492
所以我非常相信这一点，这也是我希望在未来几年里思考的一部分：这些新技术带来的积极影响。
So I'm a huge believer in that, and that's part of what I want to spend um some of my next few years thinking about as well,
the phys the impact of um positively of these new uh types of technologies.

176
00:18:11,854 --> 00:18:17,252
嗯，但这需要一些认真的创意投入，甚至可能需要一场新的运动。
Um but it won't happen without uh some serious sort of creative effort and maybe even a new movement.

177
00:18:17,808 --> 00:18:25,248
嗯，简单聊聊伦敦，我出生在伦敦北部，是地道的伦敦人。
Um, and just to talk about London and why I've always, you know, I was born in North London and you know, London, born and bred.

178
00:18:25,328 --> 00:18:36,810
我很喜欢英国和伦敦，部分原因是我在这里长大，我喜欢这种低调的、英式的风格。
但我认为我们也应该学习美国人的优点，他们很有野心，并且对自己的抱负充满信心。
and I just really love the UK and London, but partly because, you know, I grew up here and I like this a little bit understatedness or
Britishness, but I think we should also the thing the Americans do

179
00:18:36,810 --> 00:18:41,188
我很喜欢英国和伦敦，部分原因是我在这里长大，我喜欢这种低调的、英式的风格。
但我认为我们也应该学习美国人的优点，他们很有野心，并且对自己的抱负充满信心。
well is they're ambitious, and they're optimistic about their ambition.

180
00:18:41,488 --> 00:18:46,724
我认为在欧洲我们必须接受这一点，否则我们就会被抛在后面。
And that is, and we have to embrace that, I think, in Europe, otherwise we're just going to get left behind.

181
00:18:47,054 --> 00:18:49,088
这就是我试图解释关于 AI 的事情。
And this is what I'm trying to explain about the AI thing.

182
00:18:49,168 --> 00:18:53,124
不要只把它看作一个待定项目，而是去塑造它会产生什么影响。
Don't just view it as it's an it's it's to be shaped what happens with it.

183
00:18:53,390 --> 00:18:54,628
它是必然会发生的。
It's and it's gonna happen.

184
00:18:55,022 --> 00:19:02,448
所以你应该接受这个事实，然后利用你的才华在国际舞台上以积极的方式去塑造它。
So you should just make peace with that and then use your talents to shape it in a positive way on the global stage.

185
00:19:02,734 --> 00:19:06,692
你们都有能力做到这一点，在各自的领域都能产生巨大的影响。
You're all capable of doing that, you've all got big impacts in your domains.

186
00:19:07,022 --> 00:19:18,368
然后你可以争论到没完没了，但从工程师的角度来说，你最终必须拿出成果。
我曾为了蛋白质折叠问题是否能解决而和人争论了好几年。
And then you can argue to the cows come home, but actually, and this is the engineer part of me,
you just got to show it at some point, I was arguing with people for years about is the protein folding problem solvable or whatever.

187
